 🍻 Happy Friday Jr! Today's newsletter, edited by Alex Fitzpatrick, is 698 words, a 2½-min. read. Thanks to Sheryl Miller for copy editing . 

 🚨 Breaking: President Trump signed an executive order renaming Lake Ontario to "Lake America." It's unclear whether he has the authority to rename a body of water shared between countries, as he did last year when he signed a similar order changing the Gulf of Mexico to "Gulf of America." Go deeper . 

 ⚡️ Situational awareness: Trump told Axios in a phone interview today that he isn't concerned that Russia will attack NATO countries.

 Several media outlets reported that CIA Director John Ratcliffe's visit to Moscow this week was an effort to warn Russia not to attack a NATO country. But Trump disputed those reports: "I am not concerned ... at all. There is no problem." More from Barak Ravid . 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 1 big thing: Dire cyberthreat warning 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Illustration: Sarah Grillo/Axios OpenAI, Anthropic, Amazon Web Services, Microsoft and more than 100 other companies warned today there's only months to prepare for AI-enabled cyberattacks, Axios' Sam Sabin reports. 

 Hospitals, water treatment plants and other critical infrastructure will face a swarm of hacking threats as AI makes sophisticated cyber capabilities cheaper and more accessible, the group writes in an open letter. (Read.) 🛡️ The group is calling for "collective action," and urges organizations to secure critical infrastructure.

 Signatories include a range of cloud providers, cybersecurity companies, AI companies, telecoms and financial services companies, and think tanks. The organizations lay out a plan for how all companies and governments, as well as cybersecurity and AI companies, need to adapt.

 Organizations need to raise their internal security standards and fix their "highest-risk weaknesses," the group says. They also should "raise the security bar for what you buy, build, and deploy, including AI-generated code." 🤖 Cybersecurity and tech companies must quickly test and build out tools that make "AI-powered defense accessible and deployable for critical-infrastructure operators." 

 Governments need to strengthen channels for sharing actionable threat intelligence, coordinate defense at the local, national and international levels, and fund cyber defense. Frontier AI companies should give defenders access to their most capable response models during major cyber incidents, along with "significant funding, training, and hands-on support." 👾 The letter comes amid a wave of cyberattacks against critical infrastructure, including one targeting U.S. water systems with an apparent AI-generated script.

 The signatories didn't make any commitments, deadlines or specific investments. Go deeper . 

 
 
